content.thinking:
The `.then((result) => _text(result), onError: (Object error) => error)` — the mixed then/onError return types conflict (String vs Object). Use a different pattern: 

```dart
Object outcome;
try {
 outcome = _text(await tool.execute({'path': '/work/shot.png'}, source.token, null));
} on Object catch (error) {
 outcome = error;
}
``
But I need to cancel WHILE awaiting. Structure:

```dart
final completer = Completer<Object>();
unawaited(
 tool.execute({'path': '/work/shot.png'}, source.token, null).then(
   (result) => completer.complete(_text(result)),
   onError: completer.completeError,
 ),
);
await Future<void>.delayed(Duration.zero);
source.cancel();
expect(
 await completer.future.catchError((Object e) => throw e as Object) ... 
``
Hmm — simpler: 

```dart
final future = tool.execute({'path': '/work/shot.png'}, source.token, null);
await Future<void>.delayed(Duration.zero);
source.cancel();
Object outcome;
try {
 outcome = _text(await future);
} on Object catch (error) {
 outcome = error;
}
expect(outcome, isA<CancelledException>());
``
The risk: does `execute` start before `Future.delayed`? The future starts executing immediately when created (async function body runs synchronously until first await). The vision call's await chain needs to reach the MockClient handler... `await Future.delayed(Duration.zero)` gives microtasks+one timer turn. The handler registers its onCancel listener within microtasks. Then cancel → listener → throw. Good.

